::::
:::

- @Fact@: **Fitting noise** instead of signal is _not useless but actually harmful_
  - Model infers in-sample pattern that, when extrapolated out-of-sample,
    deviates from target function $\implies$ poor generalization

* Optimal Fit

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}

- The opposite of overfitting is not underfitting, but @optimal fit@, i.e., train a
  model with proper complexity for the data

- The optimal fit:
  - Implies $E_{out}$ is minimal (not $E_{in}$)
  - Does not imply generalization error $E_{out} - E_{in}$ is minimal

- The @generalization error@ is the error $E_{out} - E_{in}$ when going from
  in-sample to out-of-sample

- The degradation of fit is $\frac{E_{out} - E_{in}}{E_{in}$

::::
:::: {.column width=60%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Optimal_fit.png)

::::
:::

* Overfitting: Regression Example
- Predict diamond price as a function of carat size (regression problem)

- @True relationship@ is
  $\text{price} \sim (\text{carat size})^2 + \varepsilon$
  where:
  - Square function: larger, rarer diamonds command a price premium
  - Noise $\varepsilon$: e.g., missing features, true stochasticity

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Fit with@
  - _Line_
    - Underfit
    - High bias (large error)
    - Low variance (stable model)
  - _Polynomial of degree 2_
    - right fit
  - _Polynomial of degree 10_
    - Overfit (wiggly curve)
    - Low bias
    - High variance (many degrees of freedom)
::::
:::: {.column width=55%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L05.2.Diamond_price_example.png)

::::
:::

* Overfitting: Classification Example
- Assume:
  - You want to separate 2 classes using 2 features $x_1, x_2$
  - The true class boundary has a parabola shape

- You can use logistic regression and a decision boundary equal to:
  - _Line_: $\text{logit}(w_0 + w_1 x + w_2 y)$
    - Underfit
    - High bias, low variance
  - _Parabola: $\text{logit}(w_0 + w_1 x + w_2 x^2 + w_3 x y + w_4 y^2)$
    - Right fit
  - Wiggly decision boundary: 
    $\text{logit}(w_0 + \text{high powers of } x_1, x_2)$
    - Overfit
    - Low bias, high variance

