* Summary

- @Key idea@: overfitting happens when a model fits noise instead of signal,
  and the bias-variance decomposition explains why: $E_{out} = \text{bias}^2 +
  \text{variance}$ (plus noise for a noisy target)

- @Recap@
  - _Overfitting_: going past the model complexity that minimizes $E_{out}$
    trades in-sample fit for out-of-sample generalization
  - _Bias-variance decomposition_: $E_{out}$ splits into bias (how well
    $\calH$ can approximate $f$ on average) and variance (how much the
    learned $g$ varies across training sets)
  - _Complexity control_: regularization and other complexity knobs trade
    bias for variance; the rule of thumb $d_{VC} \approx N / 10$ matches model
    complexity to available data
  - _Learning curves_: the dual view of bias-variance analysis, showing
    $E_{in}$ and $E_{out}$ converge as the training set size $N$ grows

